  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1018 PM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nAn upper level low will be over central Nevada by 12z Tue and then \nwill move to near the Four Corners area by 12z Wed.  Will gradually \nsee a better chc of showers along with a few tstms by Tue aftn in \nthe mtns.  Across the plains, cooler temps will occur behind a front \nas weak upslope flow develops.  Highs will drop into the lower to \nmid 60 across nern CO. Any chc of showers by late aftn will be \nconfined to areas closer to the foothills.\n\nBy Tue night, will see widespread showers across the higher terrain \nwith snow levels lowering to 9500 ft overnight.  Across the plains, \nbest chc of showers overnight will be mainly along the I-25 Corridor \nwith lessor chances further east.\n\nFor Wed thru Wed night, the main question is where will the upper\nlevel low track, as it begins to move from swrn CO east northeast\ntowards ern CO. The Canadian, ECMWF and NAM have a more northern \ntrack as it moves into nern CO by 12z Thu. Meanwhile, the GFS has \na more southern track with the upper level low being over east \ncentral CO. Looking at ensemble data from the ECMWF and GFS shows\na more northern solution with the low being over nern CO. \nMeanwhile, sfc high pres will continue to build into nern CO with \nthe low level flow becoming ESE. Overall, with favorable QG ascent\nshould continue to see showers/isold tstms over the higher \nterrain thru the aftn with snow levels down to 9000 ft by late \naftn. Across nern CO, should see showers thru the aftn, especially\nalong the I-25 Corridor with a chc across the plains. Highs on \nWed, will only be in the mid 40's to lower 50's over nern CO.\n\nBy late Wed night into Thu, additional precipitation will depend on \nthe exact track of the upper level low.  If it takes a more northern \ntrack across nern CO, then best chc of precipitation will be across \nthe higher terrain along and north of I-70 and over nrn portions of \nthe plains.  Naturally, if it were to take a more southern track \nacross east central CO, then much of the area would have additional \nwidespread precipitation late Wed night thru midday Thu. \nMeanwhile snow levels Wed night into Thu morning may drop down to \n8000 ft or so. At this time, snow amounts look to be mainly in the\nadvisory range for mtn areas, except over the higher peaks which \nmay have higher amounts.\n\nFor Thu aftn into Thu night, the upper level low will weaken and \nmove east of the area. Thus, will see precipitation gradually end\nduring the aftn hours in most areas. Highs on Thu will be in the \n50's across the plains. For Thu night into Fri morning, some areas\nacross the plains may see freezing temps with frost possible in \nthose places that don't get down to freezing. \n\nLooking ahead to Fri and Sat, an upper level ridge will develop over \nthe area.  This will lead to dry conditions with a warming trend. \nHighs will rise into the upper 50's to mid 60's on Fri, over nern \nCO, and then into the lower to mid 70's by Sat.   \n\nOn Sun, a very weak upper level trough will move across the area. \nLatest cross-sections show very little moisture associated with this \nfeature. Thus it will remain dry with above normal temperatures.",
